Fan Control is for the firewall that runs its fans at full speed for ever
because the fan curve lived in the vendor's firmware and the vendor's
firmware is gone. It reads the temperatures the kernel already publishes,
decides a duty per fan, and above all hands every fan back to the hardware
the moment anything is unclear.

Nothing runs after installation, and nothing writes to a fan on the
strength of having been installed. A fresh installation watches: the page
fills with real readings - every fan, its speed, every temperature this
machine reports and the sysctl each one came from - and the chip is never
touched. That half works on any appliance, including one nobody has ever
opened.

Going further is deliberate, and it is gated by a contract of six
conditions that is re-checked before every single decision. The chip has
to be the chip the shipped hardware table describes. The table has to
permit control on that model, or the owner has to have accepted an
unmeasured entry in writing. The settings each fan was found under have to
have been captured, so they can be written back exactly. Every fan to be
driven has to have a floor - the lowest duty at which it keeps turning -
that was measured on that machine with the floor test, never typed into a
page and never taken from a datasheet. Every fan to be driven has to
report a tachometer that is turning. And every temperature the decision
needs has to be readable at that moment. If any one of them stops holding
while the controller is running, the fans go back to the hardware on that
cycle.

The curve is hysteretic, so a fan does not hunt: it speeds up at once and
slows down only after the temperature has fallen a whole hysteresis and a
dwell has passed. Above a limit temperature the curve is not consulted and
the duty is 100. Above a panic temperature the plugin stops being in the
way: it hands the fans back and exits, because a machine still climbing at
full duty has proved that this plugin is not what is keeping it cool.

Every exit hands the fans back - a clean stop, a signal, an exception, a
watchdog that fires because the loop stopped going round, the shutdown
sequence, and the removal of the package itself. A restore writes back what
was captured, never a duty someone thought looked safe. Where a restore
cannot be made, the page says so in as many words and says that only a
power cycle will fix it.

The hardware table matches on SMBIOS, carries a verify block describing the
chip that must be underneath, and says plainly how far each entry has
actually been proven. Adding an appliance is one block and a pull request,
and what is wanted in it is a measurement rather than a datasheet.

Plugin Changelog
================

1.1.2

* The page goes in the XGS section when os-xgs-npu is installed - among
  the appliance's pages, in OPNsense's order - and under System when it is
  not; os-xgs-npu moves it when it is installed or removed
* The archive an appliance downloads carries what it reads: the
  documentation, the workflows and the repository's tools left out

1.1.1

* Taking over a channel under an automatic mode (SmartFan IV): the mode
  first, then the duty, read back - before, taking control failed every
  time on such a channel (issue 3)
* Handing a channel back to an automatic mode is no longer reported as
  failed when the chip shows its curve's output
* The page's Arabic is in opnsense-arabic's catalogue; this plugin merges
  nothing into the GUI's catalogues any more

1.1

* The page and the widget in Arabic, laid out like OPNsense's own pages,
  the readings as cards and the summary on top
* A fan the board drives itself, as on the Sophos XGS 3300, is shown by its
  speed, with a working bar, and the page says it is only watched
* Sensors named, not keyed: the coprocessor readings os-xgs-npu's drivers
  copy for OPNsense's widget are dropped beside their originals, and many
  sensors of one kind fold into one card
* An update restarts a controller that only watches, so it takes effect

1.0

* Initial release
